#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166625 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd281121-21d3-5dc4-8537-af248d0fdf46" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6ddfa412-1f5a-5ddd-8d61-d7f27b106022" :question/math-academy-id "e-8751" :question/problem "For which vector field is $f(x,y) = \\sin(2x-y)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = 2\\cos(2x-y)\\,\\mathbf i -\\cos(2x-y) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166648 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "3f266f66-1d79-5864-b497-1bc1b138c318" :question/math-academy-id "e-9499" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf F = (e^{y^2} - 2xy) \\, \\mathbf i + (2xye^{y^2} - y) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf G = (3x^2 - y^2) \\, \\mathbf i + (4 - 2xy) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf H = (xy^2 + 1) \\, \\mathbf i + 2xy \\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we have $P = e^{y^2} - 2xy$ and $Q = 2xye^{y^2} - y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2ye^{y^2} - 2x, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2ye^{y^2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf G,$ we have $P = 3x^2 - y^2$ and $Q = 4 - 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -2y, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = -2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{G}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf H,$ we have $P = xy^2 + 1$ and $Q = 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf{H}$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166649 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "ae01904d-13f0-5d6e-ad3a-cc714c65765b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9500" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\arctan{3x} \\, \\mathbf j$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ the domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ and we have\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2}, \\qquad Q = \\arctan{3x}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{2a}{1 + 9x^2}, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{3}{1 + 9x^2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $\\mathbb R^2.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a}{1+9{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{1+9{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2a & =3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd281121-21d3-5dc4-8537-af248d0fdf46" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e32bf7d2-dfca-5042-a945-41727728d60a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325375} {:answer/id #uuid "bea3a6cd-e0c3-5d6f-b9a4-7e67ce4d1ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i+y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325376} {:answer/id #uuid "f996e8c6-2602-548a-a27e-228ac43c6901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325377} {:answer/id #uuid "b8de63e3-60b2-59c1-8da3-0efc2dc59cbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i-y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325378} {:answer/id #uuid "9423a753-c903-5cd2-af76-8977cf29aa41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e32bf7d2-dfca-5042-a945-41727728d60a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325375} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c1fcb6f-a289-5971-a0f7-8103c42451fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e26fb992-29b1-59c4-9818-c4b0755162ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-104396" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2x\\,i-j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd281121-21d3-5dc4-8537-af248d0fdf46" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2f0bc83-e043-553a-9983-ade6d770ebac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+2y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325382} {:answer/id #uuid "6451c6c4-cbea-5e9c-a940-f25cba83169b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325383} {:answer/id #uuid "e92a4844-d658-58ad-a83f-81f9964ac983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325384} {:answer/id #uuid "97761137-c291-506f-baac-49377bd75f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325385} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecbb9ab-b01e-5eed-8176-f6ecf72b126c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ecbb9ab-b01e-5eed-8176-f6ecf72b126c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325386} :answer-field/id #uuid "2027b169-4cf3-59a6-919d-cd605a47dcfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "990b5e9a-925b-5eb0-8bd9-597d9588fd28" :question/math-academy-id "q-104397" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd281121-21d3-5dc4-8537-af248d0fdf46" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7afc4f7d-8bec-56a7-8539-c4792a24c5f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325389} {:answer/id #uuid "2b146866-c09e-51bd-829f-07cb438d0cc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325390} {:answer/id #uuid "524ee403-7e69-5149-a4b2-c2929dde4626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325391} {:answer/id #uuid "a6330ac3-b7f9-54de-9b12-1319cd5bfc50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325392} {:answer/id #uuid "12a94eeb-3c6d-5657-8e1c-d82862e8d563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b146866-c09e-51bd-829f-07cb438d0cc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325390} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aa878f6-211a-5e0d-b48c-a11958cab1cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e81a7bc8-e770-5da6-bcc7-f363a69bd91a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104398" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y))\\,j \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd281121-21d3-5dc4-8537-af248d0fdf46" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b69b16c0-aa13-5778-b2ef-bc583efe6b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y + 1)e^{xy}i + xe^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163305} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3d328d-a7a7-526c-bbd9-76e4717393d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2}e^{xy}i + x^{2}e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163306} {:answer/id #uuid "c78bec72-c383-5d7e-b9a0-8f4e09c5580e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xye^{xy}i + (x + 1)e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163307} {:answer/id #uuid "d55039d7-cd35-55e4-ad8f-f14b8bdfbbc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2}e^{xy}i + (xy + 1)e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163308} {:answer/id #uuid "1a8f477f-887d-5565-aa72-281d9f2765d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ye^{xy}i + xye^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d55039d7-cd35-55e4-ad8f-f14b8bdfbbc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2}e^{xy}i + (xy + 1)e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163308} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa74a3cb-2703-5e63-b05a-f75c6855f983" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce34f68f-f91f-5032-a961-dafacf6d59a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104399" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})\\,j \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d299bc15-4ce0-5f6b-be6d-41a32a83e359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325396} {:answer/id #uuid "05a6edda-d0c6-5b5b-b771-8880c54ba809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325397} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b62821-566c-5ae9-89aa-0d7eadcf8cea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325398} {:answer/id #uuid "7be0721c-9ca8-59fa-a7e6-c4f261be1220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325399} {:answer/id #uuid "ad634012-1a47-55a6-9e0f-5e8e634cb31b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9b62821-566c-5ae9-89aa-0d7eadcf8cea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325398} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5459ad7-120f-5776-8a41-d80a255496bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73f6a77f-a717-55d7-89e8-d599ced23560" :question/math-academy-id "q-104410" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(ay-2x{y}^{3})\\,i+(x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,j$ on ${R}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ we have $P=ay-2x{y}^{3}$ and $Q=x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=a-6x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1-6x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-6x{y}^{2} & =1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5002d445-30a2-5c37-9d6d-26c4cd5ae402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325403} {:answer/id #uuid "fec059ee-df38-5698-9976-e1802ad55587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325404} {:answer/id #uuid "0a3e9afd-a95d-5aba-968d-07f6d8fce9cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325405} {:answer/id #uuid "85fe677e-2c64-5510-9371-a3922ba282aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325406} {:answer/id #uuid "80ff3fb1-7372-5b9c-8512-4c83826e9fbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fec059ee-df38-5698-9976-e1802ad55587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325404} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbd07589-358d-57e8-bebb-52aaa869c2ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8ed3b7d-22fa-58ba-a534-22239fadeef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-104414" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\frac{axy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{axy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{ax}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ax}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ ax & =2x \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc988d1e-536b-5f59-b865-472ba01e5c33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163317} {:answer/id #uuid "beddba6f-6486-55fc-a77a-fc625e8de38e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163318} {:answer/id #uuid "c402d630-70a8-5832-8705-ffb2cdc07958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163319} {:answer/id #uuid "26de321a-20a2-5291-8e5b-ea63b2b0159e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163320} {:answer/id #uuid "5cebda8d-5338-53eb-a058-413565f040a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cebda8d-5338-53eb-a058-413565f040a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163321} :answer-field/id #uuid "d97169ed-f827-5169-82cc-9a6ab6e2cc2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef2b9923-252b-570f-aaa0-557007eb97e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104570" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=x{e}^{xy}\\,i+y{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- $G=(xy+{y}^{2})\\,i+(xy+{x}^{2})\\,j$\n- $H=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=y{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2}{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=xy+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=xy+{x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x+2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)$ and $Q=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6adcb43b-1d9d-5c73-90ba-74c026a0206e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325410} {:answer/id #uuid "0cfc8a38-0f4d-5d3b-8e8b-0f040e1ad009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325411} {:answer/id #uuid "e87082c6-a9c0-5b7f-8ed1-50be3483f37d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325412} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6fa627-8d6c-575e-9ecc-b92670b93681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325413} {:answer/id #uuid "3f387fea-d989-5b54-9e6e-aa1eb07bdd9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cfc8a38-0f4d-5d3b-8e8b-0f040e1ad009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325411} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ee3ed99-4993-599f-a785-3436dc2f59ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40d0e8a9-cdc8-50a0-84a8-ea0e5f8e8c0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104582" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative in their respective domains?\n\n- $F=(2x-4y)\\,i+(4x-8y)\\,j$\n- $G={2}^{2x+y+1}\\,i+{2}^{2x+y}\\,j$\n- $H=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2x-4y$ and $Q=4x-8y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P={2}^{2x+y+1}$ and $Q={2}^{2x+y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={2}^{2x+y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={2}^{2x+y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)$ and $Q=\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{4xy}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4xy}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76959639-fc29-56bb-8f8c-6f3b33784e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325417} {:answer/id #uuid "e928ae70-f72e-58af-b0e2-1cc5265ff79a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325418} {:answer/id #uuid "ee311145-7687-56e6-b2d0-827baa3d799e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325419} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ac287e-d111-5afe-8740-234164806c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325420} {:answer/id #uuid "683b58d3-cc39-53da-a721-4d082dced052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76959639-fc29-56bb-8f8c-6f3b33784e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325417} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4fd1f47-ff5c-5643-b558-dba898f42079" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7f6cf45-13ae-5102-8371-f34cd4fc38c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-123984" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j$\n- $G=3{e}^{3x+2y}\\,i+2{e}^{3x+2y}\\,j$\n- $H=2xy\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and $Q=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=3{e}^{3x+2y}$ and $Q=2{e}^{3x+2y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6{e}^{3x+2y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6{e}^{3x+2y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2xy$ and $Q={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "936079d6-0fe5-5c0f-a08d-88f69431508f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163587} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d53014-d488-5a50-8c39-a20bb22fa466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163588} {:answer/id #uuid "0b5779db-4033-5686-9e13-362d4aad1030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163589} {:answer/id #uuid "29a002fc-115e-5cd3-87ac-06e3814a53c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163590} {:answer/id #uuid "79f7aaa8-e34f-5045-a463-8a1caaa6fcc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79f7aaa8-e34f-5045-a463-8a1caaa6fcc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163591} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8679fe2-8fb6-50c9-9adb-edfdd7a1c59f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86789f73-1843-5bdb-8f6f-64ce79172176" :question/math-academy-id "q-123996" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=2ay\\,i+(3-x)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=2ay,\\,Q=3-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2a,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a & =-1 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a32e640c-1a98-5f50-8aa0-ade82ba89d7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325424} {:answer/id #uuid "a455545e-c464-5f46-8eda-82717aa8271d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325425} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cc3c3f-bc88-5f3d-8612-4f7be51fbb94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325426} {:answer/id #uuid "e4578301-9d5d-5db5-86c9-a5c91732070c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325427} {:answer/id #uuid "8b393b67-f02d-5869-bafb-05bd7f8c8748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4578301-9d5d-5db5-86c9-a5c91732070c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325427} :answer-field/id #uuid "10ee2a15-f59f-5242-99b5-e7727eb0227b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7b0cb57-ecb9-5e01-9c8d-106ba5601b7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123998" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=({y}^{2}+2xy)\\,i+({x}^{2}+2axy)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP={y}^{2}+2xy,\\,Q={x}^{2}+2axy.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y+2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+2ay.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+2x & =2x+2ay \\\\ 2y & =2ay \\\\ 2y-2ay & =0 \\\\ 2y(1-a) & =0 \\\\ 1-a & =0 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4285dfa1-8551-5f40-a599-51a0ed467b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325431} {:answer/id #uuid "36d280e8-b8fa-53aa-b4ff-0f412ed1fae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325432} {:answer/id #uuid "a244e637-7702-5e05-8346-eaaf59cc281c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325433} {:answer/id #uuid "0762a1d0-0e1b-556b-9d7f-c73758ffe97f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325434} {:answer/id #uuid "07fdbc7c-655f-5ce0-971c-b721c8639985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4285dfa1-8551-5f40-a599-51a0ed467b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325431} :answer-field/id #uuid "70dbd167-ca79-5342-bfd7-99e8c9266396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4200aee3-5549-523a-9fb6-65cc885d941a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81460" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$\n- $G(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,i+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\,j$\n- $H(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2xy$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})$ and $Q=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=y{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=x{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8b109e3-a408-5571-aa89-07dd23b07e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165092} {:answer/id #uuid "e105f644-d00e-5553-a513-a02693cf421f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165093} {:answer/id #uuid "ecebcb30-be67-5010-94b5-f4a07b861906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165094} {:answer/id #uuid "76029bdc-35d4-5686-a110-4a69ec373920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165095} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb5c98f-a6a6-5e4e-8c57-cba67a58ea9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cb5c98f-a6a6-5e4e-8c57-cba67a58ea9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165096} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a86b01f-b6ec-53fc-8b0e-aad1d7fd42b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "321a61eb-7345-5f73-a2c6-72200c42218f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81745" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=({x}^{2}+2y)\\,i+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,j$\n- $G=(y{e}^{x}+2)\\,i+({e}^{x}+4y)\\,j$\n- $H=y\\,i+x\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q={x}^{2}+2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y{e}^{x}+2$ and $Q={e}^{x}+4y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=y$ and $Q=x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "993298a9-bb67-574f-9916-988c71e5b4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325438} {:answer/id #uuid "9061c7a3-2f52-5d0f-9218-2eec87a7ad4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325439} {:answer/id #uuid "f7917208-92f0-5f66-9eb9-36008dc84523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325440} {:answer/id #uuid "2b7ba93a-477a-59d7-9810-f20333580eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325441} {:answer/id #uuid "991c2b61-6e14-5e0a-9a5a-c8f1c689f4e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b7ba93a-477a-59d7-9810-f20333580eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325441} :answer-field/id #uuid "b43a1edd-5fef-58f9-b3f2-a35a92dbca6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb99568d-1f7b-590e-882c-81539cdc2e9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98946" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y)\\,i+(ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y,\\,Q=ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y & =a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd281121-21d3-5dc4-8537-af248d0fdf46" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88f81d2d-d5c5-56a4-9386-45b306a54421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyzi + x^{2}zj + (x^{2}y + 2z)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166387} {:answer/id #uuid "114c77c1-eeaa-5774-b9a8-841df8fb3176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2}yzi + x^{2}yj - (2xy + 2y)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166388} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce24d24-00cc-5dfa-ae57-7b576ed54e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-xyzi - y^{2}zj + (y^{2}x + 2y)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166389} {:answer/id #uuid "3375422a-0420-5431-9e77-b5991e7f6f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyzi - x^{2}yj + (xz^{2} + 2y)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166390} {:answer/id #uuid "3658104d-2b4f-5a06-af82-d41092238392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyzi - x^{2}j + 4z^{2}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88f81d2d-d5c5-56a4-9386-45b306a54421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyzi + x^{2}zj + (x^{2}y + 2z)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166387} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ee6962-d399-5885-8c69-5ff1918c56ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e4d7c82-2e83-562b-b6bc-3ec6c276e93c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98947" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k.\n$$"}]}
